WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.960
A human is more than two thousand times heavier than a mouse.

2
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:12.320
Does the human burn two thousand times as much energy every day?

3
00:00:13.383 --> 00:00:17.443
The first film showed why a thick, busy body pumps blood and air.

4
00:00:18.663 --> 00:00:21.343
That flow has to deliver whatever the tissue burns.

5
00:00:22.703 --> 00:00:24.223
So how much does a body burn?

6
00:00:25.267 --> 00:00:29.107
In 1883, Max Rubner asked a simple question.

7
00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:33.207
Does every gram of an animal cost the same to run?

8
00:00:34.407 --> 00:00:36.647
He studied resting dogs of many sizes.

9
00:00:37.817 --> 00:00:39.577
Dogs suit the question well.

10
00:00:40.837 --> 00:00:43.517
One species spans a wide range of sizes.

11
00:00:44.317 --> 00:00:47.757
So size can change without changing the kind of animal.

12
00:00:48.867 --> 00:00:51.887
Take one dog that is ten times heavier than another.

13
00:00:53.107 --> 00:00:56.607
If every gram cost the same, it would make ten times the heat.

14
00:00:57.887 --> 00:00:58.887
Does it?

15
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:10.340
No. Total heat rose with size, but much less steeply than mass.

16
00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:16.280
Each kilogram of a small dog made more heat than each kilogram of a big one.

17
00:01:17.283 --> 00:01:21.783
Then Rubner divided each dog's heat by its estimated body surface.

18
00:01:23.063 --> 00:01:24.903
The values came out roughly equal.

19
00:01:26.263 --> 00:01:28.183
Heat tracked surface more closely than mass.

20
00:01:29.233 --> 00:01:34.993
In 1932, Max Kleiber gathered resting rates for animals from rats to steers.

21
00:01:36.153 --> 00:01:40.813
He plotted them on logarithmic axes, where a power law becomes a straight line.

22
00:01:41.883 --> 00:01:46.963
Metabolic rate equals a constant, called a, times body mass raised to a power,

23
00:01:47.323 --> 00:01:51.643
called b. On these axes, b is the slope of the line.

24
00:01:52.717 --> 00:01:55.877
If b were one, every kilogram would cost the same.

25
00:01:57.277 --> 00:01:59.397
Kleiber's line had a slope near three quarters.

26
00:02:00.977 --> 00:02:02.897
Rubner's surface rule implies two thirds.

27
00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:08.290
Now place a mouse and a human on the widely used three-quarter fit.

28
00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:13.590
The human burns far more in total, but only a few hundred times more.

29
00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.440
Divide each by body mass.

30
00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:22.920
Each kilogram of mouse burns about seven times as much as each kilogram of human.

31
00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:27.060
That is a fitted prediction, not a single measurement.

32
00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:33.350
In most comparisons of adult mammals and birds, b is positive but below one.

33
00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:39.750
So total rate rises more slowly than mass, and rate per kilogram falls as mass

34
00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:40.750
rises.

35
00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:43.810
Treat three quarters as a fit, not a law.

36
00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:48.630
The exponent changes with the animals, their state and the method.

37
00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:51.310
Its cause is still debated.

38
00:02:52.433 --> 00:02:57.353
So across most adult mammals and birds, bigger animals burn more in total.

39
00:02:58.613 --> 00:03:00.173
But they burn less per kilogram.
